<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jost Van Dyke</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jost Van Dyke is one of the four main islands of the British Virgin Islands, lying north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortola">Tortola</a> and named after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> privateer. In the 18th century it was home to a small Quaker community, and the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-coakley-lettsom">John Coakley Lettsom</a> and the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-thornton">William Thornton</a> were born there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jost-van-dyke.jpg" alt="Jost Van Dyke" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Legis auf English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jost_van_Dyke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jost Van Dyke is the smallest of the four main islands of the British Virgin Islands, measuring roughly 8 square kilometres. It lies about 8 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortola">Tortola</a> and 8 kilometres north of Saint John in the US Virgin Islands, with Little Jost Van Dyke off its eastern end. Like many neighbouring islands it is volcanic in origin and mountainous, and its highest point is Majohnny Hill at 321 metres. The name may come from the seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> privateer Joost van Dyk, a former pirate said to have used its harbours as a hideout, though no factual evidence for this exists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The English captured the British Virgin Islands in 1672, but Jost Van Dyke seems to have been mostly ignored until the mid-eighteenth century, and a map of 1717 does not show it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> works above Great Harbour show that some cane was grown, and in 1815 140 acres were under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. The population then was 428, of whom 371 were enslaved, and by 1825 it had risen to 506. Emancipation came in 1838, and by 1853 there were 1,235 residents, 196 of whom died in a cholera outbreak that year. Afterwards people lived by small-scale fishing, farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> making, and many islanders worked at coaling wharves in St Thomas.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma">Hurricane Irma</a>, a Category 5 storm, struck in September 2017, stripped the hillsides and destroyed most of the island's homes. The 298 residents set up a recovery and command centre at Foxy's Tamarind Bar and Restaurant, which held the island's only major surviving refrigerator and generators, and used a salvaged chainsaw to clear roads until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a> repaired others later that month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jost Van Dyke had a recorded population of 298 at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, up from 140 in 1991. Tourism, especially yachting, is the mainstay of the economy. Great Harbour is one of the busiest ports in the territory, and in 2008 nearly 7,000 boats cleared through it. White Bay and the rock formation called the Bubbly Pool draw visitors, and ferries run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortola">Tortola</a>, Saint Thomas and Saint John. The architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-thornton">William Thornton</a> and the physician John C. Lettsome, founder of the Medical Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, are among the island's notable people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jost Van Dyke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following decades English settlers spread to Virgin Gorda, <b>Jost Van Dyke</b> and Anegada, and the islands were formally attached to the Leeward Islands government.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-virgin-islands">British Virgin Islands</a></li></ul></section>
